-- title:
--   D[0,∞) (§1.1, p. 5): càdlàg paths on [0, ∞), the supremum metric ‖x‖_T, and the Kolmogorov σ-algebra
-- statement:
--   This file fixes the path space of §1.1 of Reed's paper.
--
--   1. A function $x:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ is **càdlàg on $[0,\infty)$** if it is right continuous at every $t\ge 0$ and has a finite left limit $x(t-)=\lim_{s\uparrow t}x(s)$ at every $t>0$. The paper's $D[0,\infty)$ is the space of such paths; only their values on $[0,\infty)$ matter.
--   2. For a path $x$ and $T\ge 0$, the **supremum metric on $[0,T]$** is
--   $$\|x\|_T=\sup_{0\le t\le T}|x(t)|.$$
--   3. $D[0,\infty)$ is also packaged as a type whose elements are càdlàg paths, equipped with the **Kolmogorov (cylinder) σ-algebra**: the smallest σ-algebra on $D[0,\infty)$ for which every evaluation map $x\mapsto x(t)$, $t\ge 0$, is Borel measurable.
--
--   These are the objects in which Proposition 3.1 is stated: its solutions live in $D[0,\infty)$, its Lipschitz estimate is in $\|\cdot\|_T$, and its measurability claim is for the σ-algebra of item 3.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Paths are total functions $\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$; values at negative times are unconstrained and never read. The supremum is Lean's real `⨆`, which returns $0$ for an unbounded family; it is only ever applied to (differences of) càdlàg paths, which are bounded on $[0,T]$, so it is the true supremum. The paper states its measurability for the Borel σ-algebra of the Skorohod $J_1$ topology; that σ-algebra coincides with the Kolmogorov σ-algebra generated by the evaluations (Billingsley, *Convergence of Probability Measures*, 2nd ed., 1999, Theorem 16.6), an identification the paper itself uses on p. 35. The $J_1$ topology is not defined here.
-- source:
--   Reed, The G/GI/N Queue in the Halfin–Whitt Regime, arXiv:0912.2837v1, p. 5, §1.1 (D[0,∞), ‖x‖_T); p. 35, proof of Proposition 3.1 (Kolmogorov σ-field)

import Mathlib

namespace ReedGGN.Regulator

open MeasureTheory Filter Topology

/-- The paper's `D[0,∞)` (§1.1, p. 5): a path `x : ℝ → ℝ` is right continuous with left limits
on `[0, ∞)`. Only the values on `[0, ∞)` are constrained: `x` is right continuous at every
`t ≥ 0` and has a finite left limit at every `t > 0`. Values at negative times are irrelevant
everywhere in this mission. -/
def IsCadlag (x : ℝ → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ t, 0 ≤ t → ContinuousWithinAt x (Set.Ici t) t) ∧
  (∀ t, 0 < t → ∃ l : ℝ, Tendsto x (𝓝[<] t) (𝓝 l))

/-- The supremum metric on `[0, T]` (§1.1, p. 5): `‖x‖_T = sup_{0 ≤ t ≤ T} |x(t)|`.
The real `⨆` returns `0` on an unbounded family; in this mission it is only applied to
(differences of) càdlàg paths, which are bounded on `[0, T]`, so the value is the true
supremum. For `T < 0` the index set is empty and the value is `0`. -/
noncomputable def supNorm (T : ℝ) (x : ℝ → ℝ) : ℝ :=
  ⨆ t : Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T, |x t|

/-- The space `D[0,∞)` of càdlàg paths on `[0, ∞)`, as a type. -/
structure Dpath where
  /-- the underlying path -/
  path : ℝ → ℝ
  /-- it is càdlàg on `[0, ∞)` -/
  cadlag : IsCadlag path

/-- The Kolmogorov (cylinder) σ-algebra on `D[0,∞)`: the smallest σ-algebra making every
evaluation `x ↦ x(t)`, `t ≥ 0`, Borel measurable. The paper (p. 35) uses that it equals the
Borel σ-algebra of the Skorohod `J₁` topology (Billingsley 1999, Theorem 16.6). -/
instance Dpath.instMeasurableSpace : MeasurableSpace Dpath :=
  ⨆ t ∈ Set.Ici (0 : ℝ), MeasurableSpace.comap (fun x : Dpath => x.path t) inferInstance

end ReedGGN.Regulator


